1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,696
Aarav switches off the old table fan.

2
00:00:02,996 --> 00:00:06,010
Inside the switch, a
tiny spark jumps across.

3
00:00:06,310 --> 00:00:08,684
Why does switching off a fan make a spark?

4
00:00:09,184 --> 00:00:11,757
Think of a heavy water
wheel inside a pipe.

5
00:00:12,107 --> 00:00:13,007
Open the tap.

6
00:00:13,004 --> 00:00:15,188
The heavy wheel is slow to get moving.

7
00:00:15,538 --> 00:00:18,867
So the flow builds up
slowly, not all at once.

8
00:00:19,217 --> 00:00:20,155
Close the tap.

9
00:00:20,155 --> 00:00:23,102
The spinning wheel keeps the
water moving for a while.

10
00:00:23,452 --> 00:00:26,665
A coil of wire, an inductor,
behaves like that wheel.

11
00:00:27,015 --> 00:00:29,037
It fights any change in current.

12
00:00:29,037 --> 00:00:30,912
This is called self induction.

13
00:00:31,262 --> 00:00:34,901
A resistor and an inductor
together make an RL circuit.

14
00:00:35,551 --> 00:00:37,592
Let's build one on the breadboard.

15
00:00:37,942 --> 00:00:42,175
A ten volt battery, a resistor,
a coil and a two way switch.

16
00:00:42,525 --> 00:00:46,325
This change is very fast, so
we watch it in slow motion.

17
00:00:46,675 --> 00:00:47,689
Flip the switch.

18
00:00:47,689 --> 00:00:49,388
The current does not jump up.

19
00:00:49,738 --> 00:00:53,809
It rises fast at first, then
creeps up to its full value.

20
00:00:54,159 --> 00:00:56,565
The coil's voltage does the opposite.

21
00:00:56,565 --> 00:00:59,081
It starts at ten volts, then fades.

22
00:00:59,431 --> 00:01:03,393
Once the current is steady, the
coil has nothing left to fight.

23
00:01:04,043 --> 00:01:06,274
Now flip the switch the other way.

24
00:01:06,274 --> 00:01:07,630
The battery is out.

25
00:01:07,980 --> 00:01:10,078
The current does not stop at once.

26
00:01:10,078 --> 00:01:11,946
It keeps flowing the same way.

27
00:01:12,296 --> 00:01:14,746
It dies away slowly, through the resistor.

28
00:01:15,396 --> 00:01:18,075
Without that path, it has nowhere to go.

29
00:01:18,075 --> 00:01:19,391
That is the spark.

30
00:01:20,041 --> 00:01:22,029
But why does the current rise slowly?

31
00:01:22,379 --> 00:01:25,590
The battery's ten volts are shared
by the coil and the resistor.

32
00:01:25,940 --> 00:01:28,829
At the start, the current
is trying to change fast.

33
00:01:29,179 --> 00:01:31,814
So the coil pushes back
with all ten volts.

34
00:01:31,814 --> 00:01:33,285
The resistor gets none.

35
00:01:33,635 --> 00:01:36,418
As the current grows,
it changes more slowly.

36
00:01:36,768 --> 00:01:38,884
So the coil pushes back less.

37
00:01:38,884 --> 00:01:41,196
More voltage reaches the resistor.

38
00:01:41,546 --> 00:01:44,774
The closer it gets to
full, the slower it climbs.

39
00:01:44,774 --> 00:01:46,141
So the curve bends.

40
00:01:46,791 --> 00:01:48,681
How fast does the current grow?

41
00:01:48,681 --> 00:01:50,518
Again, one number decides it.

42
00:01:50,868 --> 00:01:53,939
Divide the inductance
L by the resistance R.

43
00:01:54,639 --> 00:01:57,769
This is the time constant
of an RL circuit.

44
00:01:57,769 --> 00:01:59,359
Tau equals L over R.

45
00:02:00,059 --> 00:02:04,321
After one tau, the current reaches
about sixty three percent of its full

46
00:02:04,321 --> 00:02:05,221
value.

47
00:02:05,595 --> 00:02:08,245
After five tau, it is practically full.

48
00:02:08,945 --> 00:02:11,065
A bigger L means a bigger tau.

49
00:02:11,065 --> 00:02:12,266
A heavier wheel.

50
00:02:12,616 --> 00:02:14,864
But a bigger R means a smaller tau.

51
00:02:14,864 --> 00:02:16,532
The current settles sooner.

52
00:02:17,182 --> 00:02:19,280
Now the rules, step by step.

53
00:02:19,280 --> 00:02:20,293
Growth first.

54
00:02:20,643 --> 00:02:23,825
The full current, I nought, is V over R.

55
00:02:24,525 --> 00:02:28,802
I equals I nought, times one
minus e to the minus t over tau.

56
00:02:29,502 --> 00:02:33,672
Again, e is the special number,
about two point seven one eight.

57
00:02:34,022 --> 00:02:38,615
At t equal to tau, I is zero point
six three two times I nought.

58
00:02:39,315 --> 00:02:43,542
Decay: I equals I nought, times
e to the minus t over tau.

59
00:02:44,242 --> 00:02:47,901
After one tau, thirty seven
percent of the current is left.

60
00:02:48,601 --> 00:02:50,692
Why is L over R in seconds?

61
00:02:50,692 --> 00:02:53,312
A henry is a volt second per ampere.

62
00:02:53,662 --> 00:02:55,697
An ohm is a volt per ampere.

63
00:02:56,397 --> 00:02:57,302
Divide them.

64
00:02:57,302 --> 00:02:58,925
Volts and amperes cancel.

65
00:02:58,925 --> 00:03:00,408
Only seconds are left.

66
00:03:01,058 --> 00:03:03,079
A coil fights every change.

67
00:03:03,079 --> 00:03:04,729
Tau equals L over R.

68
00:03:05,529 --> 00:03:06,536
Worked example.

69
00:03:06,536 --> 00:03:08,579
A coil of zero point five henry.

70
00:03:08,929 --> 00:03:12,874
A resistor of one hundred
ohms, and a ten volt battery.

71
00:03:13,224 --> 00:03:14,933
Tau equals L over R.

72
00:03:14,933 --> 00:03:17,821
Zero point five divided by one hundred.

73
00:03:18,171 --> 00:03:20,687
That is zero point zero zero five seconds.

74
00:03:20,687 --> 00:03:21,816
Five milliseconds.

75
00:03:22,466 --> 00:03:25,105
The full current is ten over one hundred.

76
00:03:25,105 --> 00:03:26,662
Zero point one amperes.

77
00:03:27,312 --> 00:03:31,456
After one tau, the current is zero
point six three two times that.

78
00:03:31,806 --> 00:03:33,212
About sixty three milliamperes.

79
00:03:33,862 --> 00:03:37,489
After five tau, twenty five
milliseconds, it is practically full.

80
00:03:38,139 --> 00:03:39,601
Now, the exam trap.

81
00:03:39,951 --> 00:03:43,812
Many students copy RC, and
write tau equals L times R.

82
00:03:44,162 --> 00:03:45,279
That is wrong.

83
00:03:45,279 --> 00:03:48,037
For a coil, tau equals L divided by R.

84
00:03:48,387 --> 00:03:49,411
Check the units.

85
00:03:49,411 --> 00:03:51,376
Henry divided by ohm gives seconds.

86
00:03:52,026 --> 00:03:53,404
Where is this asked?

87
00:03:53,404 --> 00:03:54,781
In PUC board papers.

88
00:03:55,131 --> 00:03:58,311
And in JEE Main, for example
in the twenty fifteen paper.

89
00:03:58,661 --> 00:04:02,283
Find tau, or the current at some
time after the switch moves.

90
00:04:02,933 --> 00:04:03,833
Try this.

91
00:04:03,717 --> 00:04:08,509
A two henry coil and a ten ohm resistor
are joined to a twenty volt battery.

92
00:04:08,509 --> 00:04:11,560
When does the current
reach half its full value?

93
00:04:11,560 --> 00:04:15,214
Take the natural log of two
as zero point six nine three.

94
00:04:20,614 --> 00:04:21,514
C.

95
00:04:21,004 --> 00:04:23,545
About zero point one three nine seconds.

96
00:04:23,895 --> 00:04:25,626
Tau equals L over R.

97
00:04:25,626 --> 00:04:29,022
Two divided by ten is
zero point two seconds.

98
00:04:29,372 --> 00:04:31,431
The full current is twenty over ten.

99
00:04:31,431 --> 00:04:32,331
Two amperes.

100
00:04:32,239 --> 00:04:33,790
Half of it is one ampere.

101
00:04:34,140 --> 00:04:38,458
So one minus e to the minus
t over tau equals one half.

102
00:04:38,808 --> 00:04:42,387
That gives t equals tau
times the natural log of two.

103
00:04:42,387 --> 00:04:45,070
About zero point one three nine seconds.

104
00:04:45,720 --> 00:04:47,267
Screenshot this card.

105
00:04:50,467 --> 00:04:53,299
Next, series RC and RL circuits on AC.

106
00:04:53,299 --> 00:04:55,724
Why does current race ahead of voltage?
